      "excluded_figures": [],
      "narrative": "Kingsmere United lead the table and are paying heavily for it: wages of £43.8m are 120% of £36.5m revenue, so the club cannot meet its payroll from income alone. Total costs of £62.0m left an operating loss of £25.5m, and the net loss was £25.8m after £2.4m of player-sale profit and £2.7m of finance costs. Losses have widened every year since 2020, when the club lost £19.6m.\n\nThe owner is covering most of the gap with fresh equity, but not enough to protect the balance sheet. Net assets have fallen from £23.2m to £5.9m in four years, debt has climbed from £28.2m to £46.5m, and current liabilities are close to double current assets. A distress score of 58 puts the club in the high-risk band.\n\nTop spot with the league's largest wage bill is exactly what that spending should buy, and £24.5m of net transfer spend over three years shows the push is still on. Promotion would change the revenue picture. Failing to go up would leave a club whose survival depends on the owner continuing to write cheques.",
