      "excluded_figures": [],
      "narrative": "Brackwater Albion are second in the table on only the fifth-largest wage bill, and they make money doing it. Revenue of £33.4m covered total costs of £30.3m for an operating profit of £3.1m, and £4.5m of profit on player sales took the net result to £6.0m. Wages take 57% of revenue.\n\nThe balance sheet is the strongest in the division. Net assets have more than doubled since 2020 to £42.3m, borrowing is just £2.2m, and £10.7m of cash on its own covers current liabilities. The distress score is zero.\n\nRevenue has risen every year, from £26.0m in 2020, and the club has still been a modest net seller, taking in £6.7m more from transfers than it spent over three seasons. That is a model built on selling well and paying sensibly. The risk is the reverse of the usual one: a club this well run, and this close to promotion, may be tempted to spend its way over the line.",
