      "excluded_figures": [],
      "narrative": "Calderbrook Athletic run close to break-even on football and make their money in the transfer market. Revenue of £27.5m against costs of £26.9m produced an operating profit of just £0.6m; profit on player sales of £6.8m, including W. Achterberg's £4.8m move to a Premier League club, turned that into a net profit of £5.8m. Over three seasons the club has taken £14.8m more from transfers than it has spent.\n\nDiscipline on wages is the other half of the model. The wage bill is £16.0m, 58% of revenue and down from 64% in 2020, yet the team sits third with only the sixth-largest payroll. Net assets have almost trebled since 2020 to £37.7m, debt is £2.9m, and current assets cover current liabilities more than three times over.\n\nThe weakness is dependence on sales: without them the club would barely break even. As long as recruitment keeps producing players other clubs want to buy, the position is secure and still improving.",
