      "narrative": "Ravensholme Town are spending to get out of the division, and the bill is showing up in the accounts. Wages of £28.6m absorb 85% of £33.7m revenue, and with total costs of £44.0m the club made an operating loss of £10.3m. Player sales of £2.5m narrowed that to a net loss of £8.5m, the largest in five years.\n\nThe club has lost money every year since 2020, and borrowing is meeting part of the cost: debt has risen from £12.5m to £17.0m while net assets have slipped from £17.0m to £12.6m. Liquidity is adequate rather than comfortable, with current assets only just covering current liabilities, and the distress score of 32 sits in the medium band.\n\nFourth place on the third-largest wage bill is slightly below what that outlay should deliver, and £16.2m of net transfer spend over three years says the club is still buying rather than selling. The strategy needs promotion to pay off. Another season of losses on this scale would push debt higher with no change in the underlying model.",
      "narrative_figures": "c66e4ee3c54e4959",
