      "narrative": "We couldn't read Westmarsh City's 2024 revenue reliably from the club's published accounts, so this year's wage-to-revenue ratio and distress score are not shown. The rest of the picture is clear enough. Wages rose to £36.8m, total costs were £55.3m, and the club recorded an operating loss of £16.1m and a net loss of £15.0m after £3.3m of player-sale profit.\n\nIn 2023, the last year we can measure, wages took 92% of revenue, up from 86% in 2020, and the net loss has grown in every one of the last five years. The balance sheet has absorbed the damage: net assets have fallen from £27.0m in 2020 to £4.8m, while debt has climbed from £22.5m to £38.5m. Current assets no longer cover current liabilities.\n\nThe second-largest wage bill in the division has bought sixth place, four positions below what that payroll should deliver. With £6.1m of net transfer spend over three years, the club is still adding cost to a squad that is underperforming it. On last year's trend, net assets will turn negative within a year unless the owner puts in capital or costs come down.",
