      "narrative": "Fennick Rovers are a small club living within their means, just. Revenue of £21.6m did not cover total costs of £23.9m, leaving an operating loss of £2.3m, but £2.9m of profit on player sales produced a net profit of £0.3m, the club's first in five years. Wages of £15.2m take 70% of revenue, down gradually from 74% in 2020.\n\nThe balance sheet is modest but sound. Net assets stand at £11.2m, borrowing is £4.1m, and current assets slightly exceed current liabilities, although cash of £1.8m leaves little room for a bad run. We couldn't read the club's 2023 net assets reliably from its published accounts, so the year-on-year movement isn't shown. The distress score of 20 is in the low band.\n\nSeventh place on the seventh-largest wage bill is exactly what the money buys. A net transfer surplus of £3.4m over three years shows the club selling to balance the books rather than to fund growth. The model is stable, but it depends on finding buyers every summer.",
